Design a basic interactive LED controller that utilizes two buttons (KEY[0] and KEY[1]) to manipulate the state of an array of LEDs (LEDG). The first button toggles the state of the least significant LED (LEDG[0]) on its rising edge, while the second button shifts the currently lit LEDs (LEDG[6:1]) one position to the left, allowing for a shift register-style effect. The design incorporates debouncing logic for the buttons through the use of delayed registers, and it utilizes a 28-bit counter to maintain timing and generate clock edges for input sampling.
